<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  apprecorder.manifest

  NOTE FOR EDITORS: a double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment, and an
  invalid manifest does not warn. The process fails to START, with
  "the side-by-side configuration is incorrect" and nothing else. Keep prose
  here free of them.

  Three of the four settings are load-bearing for accessibility, not for looks,
  which is why they live in the foundation rather than in polish:

  1. Common-Controls 6.0. Without this dependency the process gets comctl32
     version 5: the pre-XP controls, unthemed, and (the part that matters) with
     the older and thinner accessibility implementations. Every "NM_CUSTOMDRAW,
     never LVS_OWNERDRAWFIXED" decision downstream assumes v6 is active,
     because NM_CUSTOMDRAW's stage model is a v6 feature.

  2. PerMonitorV2 DPI awareness. V2, not V1: V1 leaves non-client area,
     dialogs, menus and common controls at the process's startup DPI, so a
     window dragged to a second monitor renders its chrome at the wrong size.
     For a screen-magnifier user that is not cosmetic. Declared here rather
     than called at startup because SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext must run
     before any window exists and a manifest cannot lose that race.

  3. UTF-8 active code page. The catalog is UTF-16 and the UI is wide-char
     throughout, but any ANSI CRT path (a file name through fopen, a
     GetLastError text, a third-party library) would otherwise use the system
     ANSI code page and mangle Arabic. Requires Windows 10 1903 or later, which
     is already below the floor set by process loopback (2004).

  4. longPathAware, because recordings land wherever the user keeps audio and
     MAX_PATH is not a promise worth inheriting.

  The supportedOS list is not decoration either: activeCodePage and PerMonitorV2
  are both gated on the manifest declaring Windows 10 support. Omit the GUID and
  the settings are silently ignored, which is the worst possible failure mode:
  no error, just wrong behaviour on a machine you cannot reproduce.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1"
          xmlns:asmv3="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3"
          manifestVersion="1.0">

  <assemblyIdentity type="win32"
                    name="apprecorder.app"
                    version="1.0.0.0"
                    processorArchitecture="amd64"/>

  <description>Records audio from applications and hardware inputs.</description>

  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity type="win32"
                        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
                        version="6.0.0.0"
                        processorArchitecture="*"
                        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
                        language="*"/>
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>

  <asmv3:application>
    <asmv3:windowsSettings>
      <!-- The 2005 element is the fallback for hosts that predate the 2016
           one. Both are given; the 2016 element wins where understood. -->
      <dpiAware     xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
      <longPathAware  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </asmv3:windowsSettings>
  </asmv3:application>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
      <!-- Windows 8.1 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{1f676c76-80e1-4239-95bb-83d0f6d0da78}"/>
      <!-- Windows 8 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{4a2f28e3-53b9-4441-ba9c-d69d4a4a6e38}"/>
      <!-- Windows 7 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{35138b9a-5d96-4fbd-8e2d-a2440225f93a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
